WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.647
Data quality is not a single idea.

00:00:02.647 --> 00:00:08.427
It has several dimensions. Completeness asks whether required values are present.

00:00:08.427 --> 00:00:12.777
Validity asks whether values satisfy documented rules.

00:00:12.777 --> 00:00:17.027
Uniqueness asks whether duplicate identities are controlled.

00:00:17.027 --> 00:00:21.647
Timeliness asks whether the data is current enough for the intended use.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:26.677
A dataset can perform well in one dimension and poorly in another.

00:00:26.677 --> 00:00:32.822
For example, every customer may have a phone number, so completeness is high.

00:00:32.822 --> 00:00:37.307
But if many phone numbers are invalid, validity is low.

00:00:37.307 --> 00:00:44.667
That is why it is useful to measure these dimensions separately before combining them into one overall score.

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:51.862
When someone says a dataset has ninety percent quality, ask what that number actually measures.

00:00:51.862 --> 00:00:57.909
A useful quality metric should be transparent enough that you can explain how it was calculated.
